I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{125}{3}-9)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{98}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{[\\frac{98}{3}y]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{49M}{6}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{49M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{49}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{3}}=\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435460 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435473 1069 "q-163581" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435473 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435473 1072 #uuid "a8f6939e-ee8d-5d31-921b-202a2621754c" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435473 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=4\\cdot 3=12.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[x{y}^{2}]_{x=1}^{x=5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}5{y}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}{[\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{12}(36-0) \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{x}^{2}=3\\,⇒\\,{R}_{x}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435466 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435474 1069 "q-163592" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435474 1071 "![](images/c0/c018d9df9a75b6c124b1e49b9c2359a0f8c70a5322d7ce9c04e05722005e8e8e.png)\n\nA plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in  bounded by the circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2,$ and the lines $y=x,$ $y=-x$ in the first and fourth quadrant, as shown above. Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435474 1072 #uuid "bf2295d7-f3f0-5779-bd98-5a2f23825eae" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435474 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi M}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi M}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435467 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435475 1069 "q-163575" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435475 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435475 1072 #uuid "cba3666c-1980-5c09-82de-e7dd19c40b69" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435475 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 2=6.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}[x{y}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}[{y}^{3}]_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{x}^{2}=\\frac{13}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435468 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435476 1069 "q-38823" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435476 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the lines $y=x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=8Mxy.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435476 1072 #uuid "e0566907-74aa-5b66-a065-487764f08eb0" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435476 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 8Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M[{x}^{6}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{M}{M}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186435464 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435477 1069 "q-163595" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435477 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the half of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2$ for $y\\ge 0.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{\\pi }({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435477 1072 #uuid "ed8cf354-4e1e-5975-9fd4-e963ff1f08ea" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true] [17592186435477 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{\\pi }{r}^{2}.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{8}{6}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4M}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534766 17592186045417 true]] :edb/